## TLDR Problem this solves: - Claude Code `/model` probe (`max_tokens=1`) 400s on GPT-5.x - The Anthropic gateway marks the model unavailable How it solves it: - Map the provider output-limit 400 to `stop_reason="max_tokens"` - Same for streaming and non-streaming, other errors still raise ## User Flow Before: a developer running Claude Code against LiteLLM as an Anthropic gateway cannot switch to a GPT-5.x model, because the client's availability probe comes back as an error 1. They start Claude Code with `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://litellm-domain:4000` and run `/model gpt-5.2` 2. Claude Code probes the model by sending POST http://litellm-domain:4000/v1/messages with `{"model":"gpt-5.2","max_tokens":1,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hello"}]}` 3. The gateway answers 400 with `litellm.BadRequestError: OpenAIException - Could not finish the message because max_tokens or model output limit was reached. Please try again with higher max_tokens.` 4. Claude Code reads the 400 as "this model is unavailable" and refuses the switch 5. Sending the same request by hand with `"max_tokens": 16` returns 200 with real content and `"stop_reason": "end_turn"`, so the model and the key are healthy After: the same probe comes back as an ordinary truncated turn, and the model switch goes through 1. They run `/model gpt-5.2` against the same gateway 2. Claude Code sends the identical POST http://litellm-domain:4000/v1/messages with `"max_tokens": 1` 3. The gateway answers 200 with `"stop_reason": "max_tokens"`, an empty text block, and zero `output_tokens` 4. Claude Code reads a successful response and allows the switch, then chats normally 5. The same probe with `"stream": true` returns a well-formed SSE lifecycle ending in `"stop_reason": "max_tokens"` instead of an error event 6. `"max_tokens": 16` and above behave exactly as before, returning real content and `"stop_reason": "end_turn"` 7. An unrelated bad request, for example an out-of-range `temperature`, still comes back as a 400 with the provider's message ## Relevant issues Fixes #35061 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Live proxy hitting the real OpenAI API (GPT-5.x), no mocks. Config routes the Anthropic `/v1/messages` path through chat/completions (`use_chat_completions_url_for_anthropic_messages: true`), which is the path issue #35061 hits and the one used for Azure GPT-5.x and other OpenAI-compatible reasoning backends Proxy started with ``` LITELLM_LOCAL_MODEL_COST_MAP=True python litellm/proxy/proxy_cli.py --config config.yaml --port 4000 ``` Before the fix (base `c274cf321c`), the Claude Code probe returns 400 verbatim from the issue ``` $ curl -s -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" http://localhost:4000/v1/messages \ -H "content-type: application/json" -H "x-api-key: sk-1234" \ -d '{"model":"gpt-5.2","max_tokens":1,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hello"}]}' {"error":{"message":"litellm.BadRequestError: OpenAIException - Could not finish the message because max_tokens or model output limit was reached. Please try again with higher max_tokens.. Received Model Group=gpt-5.2
Available Model Group Fallbacks=None","type":"invalid_request_error","param":null,"code":"400"}} HTTP 400 ``` After the fix (`fbbebf5044`), the same probe succeeds with `stop_reason="max_tokens"` ``` $ curl -s -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" http://localhost:4000/v1/messages \ -H "content-type: application/json" -H "x-api-key: sk-1234" \ -d '{"model":"gpt-5.2","max_tokens":1,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hello"}]}' {"id":"chatcmpl-f2856a1c-57c7-409d-8813-89747885710d","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"gpt-5.2","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":""}],"stop_reason":"max_tokens"} HTTP 200 ``` Streaming (`fbbebf5044`) emits a well-formed Anthropic SSE lifecycle ending in `stop_reason: max_tokens` ``` $ curl -s -N http://localhost:4000/v1/messages \ -H "content-type: application/json" -H "x-api-key: sk-1234" \ -d '{"model":"gpt-5.2","max_tokens":1,"stream":true,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hello"}]}' event: message_start data: {"type": "message_start", "message": {"id": "msg_5a21f61b-9a2c-4e10-b3e7-846b83d10910", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [], "model": "gpt-5.6", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}}} event: content_block_start data: {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 0, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}} event: content_block_stop data: {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0} event: message_delta data: {"type": "message_delta", "delta": {"stop_reason": "max_tokens"}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7, "output_tokens": 1}} event: message_stop data: {"type": "message_stop"} ``` Control (`fbbebf5044`): a normal budget is untouched and still finishes with real content and `stop_reason="end_turn"` ``` $ curl -s -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" http://localhost:4000/v1/messages \ -H "content-type: application/json" -H "x-api-key: sk-1234" \ -d '{"model":"gpt-5.2","max_tokens":64,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hello"}]}' {"id":"chatcmpl-E6t1fKOO6sGwyUNlRaIe5dmJ5LMJW","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"gpt-5.2","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"output_tokens":10},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hello! How can I help?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn"} HTTP 200 ``` The merge commit `75ee8d45dc` that brought the branch up to current staging did not change any of this behavior; it only moved the guard into two helpers so the call sites keep staging's single-assignment `completion_response: Final = ...` shape. The regression suite and the 596 tests under `tests/test_litellm/llms/anthropic/experimental_pass_through/` pass at that commit ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Changes Claude Code sends a `max_tokens=1` probe when switching models with `/model`. On GPT-5.x that budget is too small for the model to finish even one token, so OpenAI rejects the request with a 400 ("Could not finish the message because max_tokens or model output limit was reached"). The Anthropic `/v1/messages` chat/completions translation path in `LiteLLMMessagesToCompletionTransformationHandler` surfaced that verbatim as a `BadRequestError`, so Claude Code concluded the model was unavailable. Anthropic itself answers the same probe with HTTP 200 and `stop_reason="max_tokens"`, so the gateway should too Both the async and sync handlers now go through a wrapper around the completion call that catches that specific provider 400 (identified by the output-limit marker in the message). When it matches, it synthesizes an empty `finish_reason="length"` `ModelResponse` and feeds it through the existing translation path, which already maps `length` to Anthropic `max_tokens`; for streaming it wraps that response in `MockResponseIterator` so the same SSE adapter produces the normal message lifecycle. Any other `BadRequestError` re-raises unchanged, so unrelated failures keep their current behavior ``` async def _acompletion_allowing_max_tokens_truncation(completion_kwargs, model, stream): try: return await litellm.acompletion(**completion_kwargs) except Exception as e: if not _is_output_token_limit_error(e): raise truncated = _build_max_tokens_truncation_response(model) # empty, finish_reason="length" return MockResponseIterator(model_response=truncated) if stream else truncated ``` The sync helper is the same shape over `litellm.completion`, and each call site stays a single `completion_response: Final = ...` assignment Regression coverage lives in `tests/test_litellm/llms/anthropic/experimental_pass_through/adapters/test_handler_max_tokens_truncation.py` and asserts the async/sync non-streaming responses carry `stop_reason="max_tokens"`, the streaming path emits the `max_tokens` SSE, and an unrelated `BadRequestError` still propagates ## Caveats (if any) - Overlaps #36859, which maps the same 400 in the OpenAI/Azure helpers - If that lands, this only backstops other providers hitting the same error - `choices=[...]` needs `mutable-ok`: `ModelResponse.__init__` ignores non-list choices ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR Link to Devin session: https://app.devin.ai/sessions/a1b43c1b83a14a26801c3dcb35b11959

