## TLDR Problem this solves: - Callers could override the deployment's provider key from the body - `api_base` needed admin opt-in, `api_key` needed none - Any caller's key silently signed the upstream call How it solves it: - Ban body `api_key` on LLM routes, like `api_base` - Both existing admin opt-ins still allow it - Control-plane routes that take a body `api_key` keep working ## User Flow Before: on a proxy where the admin never enabled clientside credentials, anyone who can reach the gateway picks which provider key it bills 1. The admin runs the proxy with one deployment, `gemini-flash` mapped to `gemini/gemini-2.5-flash` with the key held in `config.yaml`, and no clientside credential settings anywhere 2. A caller sends POST http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1/chat/completions with `{"model":"gemini-flash","messages":[...],"api_key":"canary_invalid_gemini_key"}` and gets back HTTP 401 carrying Google's own `"reason": "API_KEY_INVALID"`, so the caller's string, not the admin's key, was the one Google saw 3. The same caller swaps in a Gemini key of their own and the request returns HTTP 200 with a real completion, still on the admin's deployment 4. The same caller sends `"api_base":"https://example.com"` instead and gets HTTP 401 `Rejected Request: api_base is not allowed in request body`, so the guard exists, it just skips the credential 5. Anyone who can reach this gateway can therefore pin every request to a provider account of their choosing, dodging the admin's key, its quota, and its spend attribution After: the same body field is refused unless the admin opted in, so the deployment's key is the only one that can be used 1. The admin runs the exact same config 2. The caller sends the same POST http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1/chat/completions with `"api_key":"canary_invalid_gemini_key"` and now gets HTTP 401 `Rejected Request: api_key is not allowed in request body`, naming the two settings an admin would have to turn on. Nothing is sent to Google 3. The caller swaps in a valid Gemini key of their own and gets the same refusal, so a working key is no more accepted than a bogus one 4. A request with no `api_key` in the body still returns HTTP 200 with a real completion, unchanged 5. An admin who wants the old behavior sets `allow_client_side_credentials: true`, or `configurable_clientside_auth_params: ["api_key"]` on that deployment, and the caller's body key is honored again 6. A caller who cannot get the admin to flip either setting can no longer choose which provider account serves their request ## Relevant issues Fixes #36794 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Live proxy, no mocks, real Gemini calls costing real $. Reporter's config verbatim, no `general_settings`, no master key, no DB: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: gemini-flash litellm_params: model: gemini/gemini-2.5-flash api_key: os.environ/GEMINI_API_KEY ``` Every case below ran 5 times with identical results. ### Before, at `5f2986a1f3` (parent commit) ```bash curl -s -w '
