## TLDR Problem this solves: - Realtime client secrets fail for aliased model groups - The pre-routing group name still rides inside `session` - Router picks the deployment, then the alias wins How it solves it: - Router rewrites `session.model` to the routed deployment - Sessions without a model stay untouched - Nested transcription models are preserved ## User Flow Before: a developer whose voice app mints ephemeral realtime keys through the gateway cannot use a model group whose name differs from the underlying model 1. Their proxy admin serves a group named `my-realtime-group` backed by `openai/gpt-realtime-2.1-mini` 2. The app sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/realtime/client_secrets with `{"model": "my-realtime-group"}` 3. Back comes HTTP 400 saying `LLM Provider NOT provided ... You passed model=my-realtime-group`, so the browser never gets a key 4. Sending the same call with `"session": {"type": "realtime", "model": "my-realtime-group"}` fails identically 5. Only the workaround of blanking the model, `"session": {"model": ""}`, returns a secret, and asking for the group by its real model string works, so the app has to hardcode provider model names After: the same request mints a real ephemeral key bound to the group's deployment 1. Their proxy admin serves the same group named `my-realtime-group` backed by `openai/gpt-realtime-2.1-mini` 2. The app sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/realtime/client_secrets with `{"model": "my-realtime-group"}` 3. Back comes HTTP 200 with an ephemeral `value` and `session.model` reading `gpt-realtime-2.1-mini`, so the browser connects 4. Sending the same call with `"session": {"type": "realtime", "model": "my-realtime-group"}` also returns 200 with the resolved model 5. No workaround needed, and a session carrying only transcription settings keeps them as sent ## Relevant issues Fixes #36742 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [