## TLDR Problem this solves: - `redact_user_api_key_info: true` was a no-op for OTel - Traces and metrics leaked key hash, user email, team, org - Same leak on /v1/messages, /v1/chat/completions, /v1/responses How it solves it: - Route OTel span metadata through the same redaction helper langfuse uses - Also redact the metric attributes and the team tags on child spans - Regression tests read the exported span and metric attributes ## User Flow Before: an operator who turned redaction on still sees their users' emails, ids, and key hashes on every span in Jaeger 1. They set `redact_user_api_key_info: true` and `success_callback: ["otel"]` in the proxy config, point `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` at their collector, and restart the proxy 2. They send POST http://localhost:4000/v1/messages with a virtual key belonging to a user and a team, and get HTTP 200 with a normal completion 3. They open the Jaeger UI at http://localhost:16686, pick service `litellm`, and open the newest trace 4. The `litellm_request` span still shows `metadata.user_api_key_hash`, `metadata.user_api_key_user_id`, `metadata.user_api_key_user_email`, `metadata.user_api_key_team_id`, `metadata.user_api_key_team_alias`, `metadata.user_api_key_org_id`, and the per-key spend and budget values 5. They repeat step 2 against /v1/chat/completions and /v1/responses and find the same tags on those spans too 6. Anyone with read access to the tracing backend can pull the full list of users, their emails, and their key hashes out of the traces After: the same operator sees the same traces with the key-identity tags gone, and nothing else about the span changes 1. They set `redact_user_api_key_info: true` and `success_callback: ["otel"]` in the proxy config, point `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` at their collector, and restart the proxy 2. They send POST http://localhost:4000/v1/messages with a virtual key belonging to a user and a team, and get HTTP 200 with a normal completion 3. They open the Jaeger UI at http://localhost:16686, pick service `litellm`, and open the newest trace 4. The `litellm_request` span carries no `metadata.user_api_key_*` tag at all, while `litellm.model_group`, `litellm.provider.model`, `gen_ai.usage.*`, `gen_ai.cost.total_cost`, and `http.route` are all still there 5. Repeating step 2 against /v1/chat/completions and /v1/responses gives the same clean spans 6. A viewer of the tracing backend can no longer tie a trace to a user, an email, or a key, so traces can be shared and retained ## Relevant issues Fixes #36758 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Both legs ran against a live proxy on random unused ports, with a postgres-backed virtual key bound to a user (email `nome.cognome@company.com`) and a team, real Anthropic `claude-sonnet-4-6` calls costing real money, OTLP gRPC export into Jaeger 1.58, and the span tags read back through Jaeger's own UI and HTTP API. The reporter's Bedrock model was substituted because the available AWS credentials are denied `bedrock:InvokeModel` on that inference profile; the leak lives in the shared OTel logging path, not in a provider Common setup for both legs: ```bash docker run -d --name jaeger-36758 -p 16686:16686 -p 4317:4317 jaegertracing/all-in-one:1.58 cat > config